 "lq": [
  {
   "_id": 12731,
   "ANSWER": "<p data-end=\"660\" data-start=\"528\"><strong data-end=\"548\" data-start=\"528\">Frame allocation</strong> refers to how the operating system distributes <strong data-end=\"632\" data-start=\"596\">available physical memory frames</strong> among <strong data-end=\"659\" data-start=\"639\">active processes</strong>.(20%)</p>\n\n<p data-end=\"760\" data-start=\"662\">The goal is to ensure <strong data-end=\"696\" data-start=\"684\">fairness</strong>, <strong data-end=\"722\" data-start=\"698\">minimize page faults</strong>, and <strong data-end=\"759\" data-start=\"728\">optimize system performance</strong>.</p>\n\n<hr data-end=\"765\" data-start=\"762\" />\n<h3 data-end=\"815\" data-start=\"767\"><strong data-end=\"815\" data-start=\"774\">Types of Frame Allocation Strategies:</strong></h3>\n\n<hr data-end=\"820\" data-start=\"817\" />\n<h4 data-end=\"851\" data-start=\"822\">1. <strong data-end=\"851\" data-start=\"830\">Equal Allocation:</strong>(20%)</h4>\n\n<ul data-end=\"1039\" data-start=\"852\">\n\t<li data-end=\"931\" data-start=\"852\">\n\t<p data-end=\"931\" data-start=\"854\">Every process gets the <strong data-end=\"902\" data-start=\"877\">same number of frames</strong>, regardless of size or need.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"954\" data-start=\"932\">\n\t<p data-end=\"954\" data-start=\"934\">Simple to implement.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"1039\" data-start=\"955\">\n\t<p data-end=\"1039\" data-start=\"957\">\u274c May lead to <strong data-end=\"987\" data-start=\"971\">inefficiency</strong> for large processes and <strong data-end=\"1023\" data-start=\"1012\">wastage</strong> for small ones.</p>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p data-end=\"1053\" data-start=\"1041\"><strong data-end=\"1053\" data-start=\"1041\">Example:</strong></p>\n\n<ul data-end=\"1122\" data-start=\"1054\">\n\t<li data-end=\"1122\" data-start=\"1054\">\n\t<p data-end=\"1122\" data-start=\"1056\">If 100 frames are available and 5 processes &rarr; each gets 20 frames.</p>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr data-end=\"1127\" data-start=\"1124\" />\n<h4 data-end=\"1165\" data-start=\"1129\">2. <strong data-end=\"1165\" data-start=\"1137\">Proportional Allocation:&nbsp;</strong>(20%)</h4>\n\n<ul data-end=\"1285\" data-start=\"1166\">\n\t<li data-end=\"1226\" data-start=\"1166\">\n\t<p data-end=\"1226\" data-start=\"1168\">Frames are allocated <strong data-end=\"1210\" data-start=\"1189\">based on the size</strong> of the process.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"1285\" data-start=\"1227\">\n\t<p data-end=\"1285\" data-start=\"1229\">A process with larger memory needs gets <strong data-end=\"1284\" data-start=\"1269\">more frames</strong>.</p>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p data-end=\"1299\" data-start=\"1287\"><strong data-end=\"1299\" data-start=\"1287\">Formula:</strong></p>\n\n<pre data-end=\"1395\" data-start=\"1300\">\n<code>Frames for Pi = (Size of Pi / Total size of all processes) &times; Total available frames </code></pre>\n\n<p data-end=\"1436\" data-start=\"1397\">More efficient than equal allocation.</p>\n\n<hr data-end=\"1441\" data-start=\"1438\" />\n<h4 data-end=\"1481\" data-start=\"1443\">3. <strong data-end=\"1481\" data-start=\"1451\">Priority-Based Allocation:&nbsp;</strong>(20%)</h4>\n\n<ul data-end=\"1602\" data-start=\"1482\">\n\t<li data-end=\"1545\" data-start=\"1482\">\n\t<p data-end=\"1545\" data-start=\"1484\">Processes with <strong data-end=\"1518\" data-start=\"1499\">higher priority</strong> are given <strong data-end=\"1544\" data-start=\"1529\">more frames</strong>.</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"1602\" data-start=\"1546\">\n\t<p data-end=\"1602\" data-start=\"1548\">Used in <strong data-end=\"1569\" data-start=\"1556\">real-time</strong> or <strong data-end=\"1593\" data-start=\"1573\">mission-critical</strong> systems.</p>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<hr data-end=\"1607\" data-start=\"1604\" />\n<h4 data-end=\"1648\" data-start=\"1609\">4. <strong data-end=\"1648\" data-start=\"1617\">Global vs Local Allocation:&nbsp;</strong>(20%)</h4>\n\n<ul data-end=\"1815\" data-start=\"1649\">\n\t<li data-end=\"1737\" data-start=\"1649\">\n\t<p data-end=\"1737\" data-start=\"1651\"><strong data-end=\"1673\" data-start=\"1651\">Global Allocation:</strong> A process can take frames from <strong data-end=\"1720\" data-start=\"1705\">any process</strong> (shared pool).</p>\n\t</li>\n\t<li data-end=\"1815\" data-start=\"1738\">\n\t<p data-end=\"1815\" data-start=\"1740\"><strong data-end=\"1761\" data-start=\"1740\">Local Allocation:</strong> A process is <strong data-end=\"1797\" data-start=\"1775\">limited to its own</strong> allocated frames.</p>\n\t</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p data-end=\"1890\" data-start=\"1817\">\u2705 Global &rarr; better overall usage<br data-end=\"1851\" data-start=\"1848\" />\n\u274c Global &rarr; higher risk of <strong data-end=\"1890\" data-start=\"1877\">thrashing</strong></p>\n\n<hr data-end=\"1895\" data-start=\"1892\" />\n<h3 data-end=\"1959\" data-start=\"1897\">&nbsp;</h3>\n\n<p>&nbsp;</p>\n\n<ul data-end=\"1270\" data-start=\"1186\">\n</ul>\n",
   "COURSE_CODE": "21CSC202J",
   "LEVEL": 1,
   "OUTCOMES": [
    "PLO4"
   ],
   "QUESTION_DESC": "<p>Explain the different strategies for allocation of frames in memory management.</p>\n",
   "SESSION_ID": 409,
   "TYPE": "lq",
   "key": 12731
  }
 ]
